## Best Nail Shapes for Short September Nails

What's the best nail shape for short nails? Squoval, hands down. The flat tip distributes pressure evenly across the nail, and the softened corners remove the exact spot where square nails chip and almond nails snap. Round comes in a close second for anyone who wants zero corners at all.

<strong>Squoval</strong> is a nail shape that combines the straight sides and flat tip of square with the gently rounded corners of oval, and it is the shape nail technicians reach for most often when a client's length is already short. Almond, the shape that dominates most long-nail September inspiration, actually struggles at short length. It relies on a tapered point that needs extra millimetres to read as elegant rather than stubby, and that same taper is the weakest, most breakage-prone part of the nail.

Round works nearly as well for durability, trading squoval's slightly graphic flat tip for a softer, more forgiving curve. It suits anyone who has broken a nail recently and wants the gentlest possible shape while it grows back. Square, meanwhile, is the one shape worth approaching carefully at short length. Its sharp ninety-degree corners look crisp on longer nails but catch on fabric and hair when there's less nail bed to support them, and that's exactly where chips start.

The takeaway for shape shopping this September: pick squoval as your default, keep round on the shortlist if your nails are on the fragile side, and save almond and true square for when you have a bit more length to work with.

