## Pattern and Print Ideas That Work on Short Nails

A pattern doesn't need ten repeats to register. It needs three, spaced evenly, and a short nail is exactly the right size to prove it.

For <strong>short september nails</strong>, polka dots are the most forgiving print for a small canvas, since a dotting tool naturally produces a consistent, small-scale mark that doesn't need adjusting for less space. Three or four dots per nail, staggered rather than lined up, gives a playful print without crowding the tip. Plaid, done as two or three intersecting fine lines rather than a full woven grid, reads as autumnal without asking for detail work a short nail can't hold.

Terrazzo-style fleck patterns work well too, since irregular flecks in cream, rust, and olive scale down naturally without losing their randomness. The one print to approach carefully is anything with a repeating linear motif designed for a long nail bed, like a detailed floral vine. Those need real adaptation rather than a straight shrink, which the next section covers directly.

