## Micro-French Tips for September

A French tip has never needed length. It needs a clean line, and a short nail draws that line just as crisply as a long one.

The <strong>micro-French tip</strong> is a French manicure technique that uses a noticeably thinner tip line, usually one or two millimetres, scaled specifically for shorter nail beds where a traditional wide French tip would overwhelm the available space. Where a classic French tip covers close to a third of the nail, the micro version sits right at the free edge, leaving the rest of the nail visually longer and cleaner.

September's take on <strong>short september nails</strong> in micro-French leans warm. Swap the usual bright white tip for a soft caramel, terracotta, or even a deep espresso line over a sheer nude base, and the effect reads seasonal rather than bridal. A single fine gold thread traced just above the tip line adds a small seasonal detail without adding bulk.

The technique works best with a thin, angled detail brush rather than the wide brush used for a standard French manicure. Steady, single-stroke application matters more here than on a longer nail, since there's less room to correct an uneven line after the fact.

