## Short Square vs Short Round: Which Should You Choose

Square asks for confidence. Round asks for nothing at all, and that difference is the whole decision.

Short square keeps the flat tip and sharp corners intact, just scaled down. It photographs cleanly and pairs especially well with bold colour or a graphic French line, since the flat edge gives those designs a crisp border to sit inside. The tradeoff is exactly what shows up in the durability section above: those corners are the first place a nail chips, and short square needs more frequent touch-ups to stay looking sharp rather than worn.

Short round removes that tradeoff entirely. There is no corner to catch, snag, or chip, which makes it the lowest-maintenance option on this list and the safest choice for anyone easing back into longer nails after a breakage. What it gives up is some of square's graphic quality. Rounded tips soften every design slightly, which suits minimal and French looks more naturally than bold geometric ones.

Neither shape is a wrong choice for <strong>short september nails</strong>. Square suits the reader who wants definition and doesn't mind more frequent filing. Round suits the reader who wants a set that survives the month with minimal fuss. Squoval, as covered earlier, remains the default for anyone who wants a bit of both.

